# Exact cached post-live --all findings; answers explain only the anchors of their class.

[step] This step is the complete source cover's rear-cut-edge parking pad on S25R, top Z 10.50 above the receiver's Z 10.06 top. It is storage outside the active sliding channel, bearing only the cover's cut edge in its relaxed open pose. The raised pad is not a slider clearance rung and carries no retention or inversion qualification.
alternatives: A deeper solid parking buttress can preserve the same cut-edge bearing height by extending below/outward, but adds stock around the accessible cover lip. Lowering the pad flush changes the parked cover pose and its surveyed clearance. A separate broad tray parks the cover independently and increases the sculpture footprint.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/s25r-sliding-receiver.step
click: x=-13.600 y=7.900 z=10.500

[ceiling] These flat ceilings are the slider's functional body and wing retaining faces. The body ceilings are Z 7.06 over the Z 3.00 floor; the wing ceilings are Z 5.38. With the matching tongue seated 0.25 mm above the floor, both retain 0.45 mm nominal roof air while the annotated X air changes per face. The lane is open at both Y ends and through its central inspection window for automatic support removal. Preserve those faces to compare the same flat-wing slide; actual friction and support removal remain unmeasured.
alternatives: Sloping the ceilings or opening the retaining caps eliminates their flat print-down faces but changes vertical play and wing capture. A receiver assembled around the tongue from two larger open halves prints those faces on the bed, but adds a joint and a different alignment/stiffness contribution to the fit. More roof stock can be added outward without changing air, but enlarges the displayed lane and its support envelope.
